AB  - In urban warfare or for surveillance purposes, having a stand-off radar system with the capability of "seeing" behind corners would be of great importance. The possibility of detecting moving people and objects in the shadow region of a street corner using diffraction and multipath propagation of radar waves has previously been demonstrated in experimental set-ups. Detections of movements in a realistic scene are investigated in this study.The measurements were made using a radar operating at X-band (9.95-10.05 GHz) in a stepped-frequency mode. In these experiments a semi-monostatic single receiver-transmitter radar system was used as a data collector. The moving objects in the measurements are persons walking, riding a bicycle and driving a car. All detections were made after Doppler filtering, using coherent subtraction between different frequency sweeps. An adaptive detection threshold has been applied and the detections can be shown in range vs. time plots, where the movement of the object can been seen as detection tracks.Detections of movements are made fairly easily. Target returns after one and two wall reflections before the target, i.e., in total five reflections between transmission and receiving, can be identified. No attempt to exploit diffraction was made in these measurements. The background is generally well suppressed by the signal processing. Simultaneous detection of two objects moving in the scene can be made, when allowed by the range resolution. The results are promising with low false alarm rates. Future work will address positioning and micro-Doppler analysis of the measurement data.
