TY  - JOUR
AU  - Bitarafan, Mahdi
AU  - Nemati, Alireza
TI  - Constructed urban infrastructure safety prediction under explosive effects using hybrid explainable ML approaches
T2  - STRUCTURES
M3  - Article
AB  - The act of urbicide has increased recently all over the world, resulting in the destruction of cities and their urban infrastructures. However, evaluating the vulnerability of urban structures to explosions remains challenging due to nonlinear structural responses and uncertainty in blast phenomena. While numerical and experimental models have advanced understanding under uncertain conditions, they often simplify the interplay between multiple structural and environmental factors that dominate damage severity. This study develops an explainable datadriven probabilistic framework to evaluate the reliability of buildings under explosive effects by capturing complex interactions among structural, architectural, and environmental variables based on a newly compiled multi-factor dataset. A hybrid ensemble of Random Forest and XGBoost, enhanced through SMOTE and ADASYN resampling, was implemented to improve predictive generalization across rare high-damage events. The model achieved high accuracy (R2 = 0.8828) and interpretability through SHAP-based feature attribution and sensitivity analysis. Results, supported by scenario simulations, indicate that structural damage occurs within a probabilistic range from minor to near-collapse states depending on explosive yield, distance, type of attack, and shielding conditions. Two to three intervening building blocks substantially reduce the likelihood of severe damage, while explosive amounts exceeding 2000 kg TNT cause saturation in failure probability. Finally, comparison with the classical Hopkinson-Cranz scaling law confirmed the consistency and validity of the proposed model.
